course: dictionary
lesson: 3064
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Economics is a long word, but its context gives you clues. «Science, maths and economics all grew out of philosophy long ago.» Science and maths are school subjects, so economics is probably a subject too. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: studying how money and trade work also grew out of philosophy long ago, and that still makes sense.
explain: The word can also mean the money side of a plan or business. In «The economics of the plan» the word «plan» tells you that economics here is about the money. A plan can look good on paper, but its economics may not work, which means the money does not add up.
explain: Sometimes a phrase is an old name for a lesson. «Home economics» was the old name for lessons in cooking and running a house. The words around the phrase, like «home», and what you know about school help you understand it.
fact: «Economics» means the study of how people, businesses and countries make, buy, sell and use money, goods and services.
fact: «Economics» can also mean the money side of a plan or business.
fact: «Economics» is uncountable, so you do not say «an economics» or «economics many»; it takes a singular verb: «Economics is useful.»
fact: «Home economics» is an old name for school lessons in cooking and household tasks.
fact: Context clues, like the words «science» and «maths» beside a word, help you work out which meaning fits.
exercise: «Science, maths and economics all grew out of philosophy long ago.» In this sentence, economics is | A type of boat | A kind of food | *A school subject about money, goods and services | A sports team
exercise: Ben says, «The economics of the plan don't work.» What does economics mean here? | A kind of map | The name of the person who made the plan | A short, quick talk | *The money side of the plan
exercise: Choose the right verb: «Economics ___ my favourite lesson this year.» | have, because it is a plural noun | were, but only when there are many | are | *is
test: Which sentence uses economics correctly? | *Economics is hard for me. | The economics is in the box. | Economics have many rules. | Economics are hard for me.
test: In «home economics», what does economics mean? | *Lessons in cooking and running a household | The study of stars | A number puzzle with no answer | A kind of bank account
exam: Which meaning fits «economics» in «The economics of the family's new shop were carefully planned»? | The name of a street | *The money side of the shop | Feelings about being happy | A kind of food
